## Cystic diseases of the kidney

### Adult polycystic kidney disease

- **Definition** - the most common inherited kidney disorder characterised by development of many small cysts that enlarge slowly and irregularly
- **Genetics:**
  - Inheritance - Autosomal dominant pattern of PKD1 gene (85%) or PKD2 gene (15%)
- **Epidemiology** - the most common hereditary kidney disorder:
  - Prevalence - 1:1000
  - Burden - estimated 5-10% patients on RRT have adult PKD:
    - PKD1 mutations - 50% develop ESRD with earlier onset (median age 52y)
    - PKD2 mutations - only minority develop ESRD with later onset (median age 69y)
- **Clinical features:**
  - **Asymptomatic** - most remain asymptomatic until later in life
  - **Hypertension** - most develop HTN from the age 20y and onwards, and will be present in basically all patients once CKD ensues
  - **Ballotable kidney** - nodular ballotable kidney(s) on one or both sides
  - **Renal manifestations:**
    - Vague abdominal/ loin discomfort - due to increasing mass of renal tissues
    - Acute loin pain and renal colic - due to infections, rupture, or haemorrhage of cysts
    - Haematuria - often with little to no proteinuria
    - Urinary tract and cystic infections
  - **Extrarenal manifestations:**
    - Hepatic cysts (30% patients) - LFT rarely derranged, but can cause massive, symptomatic hepatomegaly
    - Berry aneurysms - 10% patients develop subarrachnoid patients
    - Valvular disease - a/w mild MR, AR
    - Colonic diverticula and abdominal wall hernias
- **Ix:**
  - **Ultrasound kidneys** (95% sensitivity) - demonstration of multiple cystic lesions in patients \> 20y (may miss small cyst in young individuals)
  - **Molecular diagnosis** - possible not not in routine clinical practice
  - +/- Non-contrast MRI abdomen
  - \+*- MR angiography - screening of aneurysm can be done in families w* Hx of subarachnoid haemorrhage (not usually indicated)
- **Dx** - clinical Dx (compatible Hx and P/E, +ve FHx, US findings)
- **Mx** - manage as CKD:
  - BP control - reduced CVS morbidity and mortality, no evidence of effects on renal function deterioration
  - V2 receptor antagonist (tolvaptan) - some evidence in showing slow down in cyst formation
  - RRT - generally good candidates for dialysis and transplantation

### Medullary Sponge Kidney Disease

### Medullary Cystic Kidney Disease

## Renal stone disease

- **Definition** - Formation of crystal aggregates occuring anywhere from the uretero-pelvic juntion to the bladder
- **Types of renal stone** - 90% of stones are calficied and hence radio-opaque:
  - Calcium oxlate (60%)
  - Calcium phosphate (15%)
  - Uric acid (10%)
  - Magnesium ammonium phosphate/ struvite (15%) - a/w ascending UTI
  - Cystine and others (1%)
- **Etiology/ Risk factors of renal stones:**
  - Dietic factors - low urine volume (low intake, excessive sweating), high protein, high sodium, low calcium
  - Altered urinary solute and colloids - High sodium excretion, oxlate excretion, urate excretion, citrate excreation
  - Acquired causes - Hypercalcaemia of any causes (e.g. primary hyperparathyroidism), ileal disease or resection, distal (type 1) RTA
  - Congenital causes:
    - Familial hypercalciuria
    - Cystinuria
    - Distal RTA
    - Primary hyperoxaluria
    - Medullary sponge kidneys
- **Clinical features of renal stones** - highly variable:
  - Asymptomatic - most patients with renal stone disease are asymptomatic
  - Renal colic - acute onset of severe loin pain caused by ureteric peristalsis against obstruction:
    - Site - flank radiating to anterior abdominal wall
    - Onset - acute in onset, increase to maximal intensity within few minutes
    - Character - colicky type pain, on top of a background of dull loin pain
    - Radiation - round the flanks towards groin, testes/ labia due to descent of stone
    - Associated S/S - N/V, sweating, pallor due to excruciating pain
    - Exacerbating and relieving factor - constant, restless patient unable to obtain relief through posture changes or pacing around room
    - Severity - often excruciating (severity not related to size of stone)
  - Haematuria - usually macroscopic in small amounts accompanying pain
  - UTI
  - Urinary tract obstruction
- **Ix and Diagnostic evaluation of renal stone disease:**
  - **Aims of Ix:**
    - Identification of stone
    - Determine whether stone is causing obstruction
    - Workup for secondary causes of stone
  - **Imaging** - identification of stone and determining ureteric obstruction:
    - KUB X Ray - identification of radio-opaque stones (90%)
    - US Kidney - dilatation of renal pelvis and ureters (hydronephrosis and hydroureteres) demonstrating ureteric obstruction
    - +/- CT/KUB or IVU - more sensitivy due to identification of radio-lucent stones (e.g. uric stones, cystine stones)
  - **Labs** - extent of workup of cause dependent on whether this is the 1st or recurrent stone:
    - <u>Routine Ix</u> - for all instances of renal stones:
      - Urine dipstick - for protein, blood and glucose
      - Bloods - RFT (Urea, Creatinine, eGFR, Bicarbonate), CaPO4, Uric acid +/- PTH (if Ca high)
    - <u>Additional Ix</u> - for young patients, or recurrent stones:
      - Stone chemical analysis - defines underlying type of stone
      - 24h urine - for urea, CCr, Sodium, calcium, oxalate, uric acid
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - Symptomatic relief:
      - Analgesics - requires powerful analgesia:
        - Regimen - oral diclofenac (100 mg), +/- IM morphine (10-20 mg) or pethidine (100 mg)
      - Antiemetics - for vomiting
    - Removal of stones:
      - Watchful waiting - 90% of stones \< 4mm in diameter pass spontaneously
      - Surgical Tx - in persistent stones, or other complications or comorbidities
    - Patient education on measures to prevent future stones
  - **Surgical Mx** - requires ABx cover prior to and after surgery:
    - **Indications of Surgical Intervention:**
      - Pain and persistence of stone
      - Stone in solitary kidney
      - Pyonephrosis a/w stone
      - Obstruction +/- anuria a/w stone
    - **Modalities of Surgical Interventions** - procedure depends on site of stone:
      - Renal pelvis/ kidney - Extracorpeal shock wave lithotripsy (ESWL), Percutaneous nephrolithotomy (PCNL), Uteroscopy
      - Upper ureters - ESWL, Uteroscopy
      - Lower ureter - EWSL, Uteroscopy
      - Bladder - Cytoscopic/ Endoscopic/ Open removal or destruction

## Isolated defects of tubular function

- **Definition** - heterogenous groups of disorders that are caused by specific defects in transport molecules expressed in renal tubular cells
- **Examples of isolated defects of tubular functions:**
  - <u>Renal glycosuria</u> - benign autosomal recessive defect of tubular reabsorption of glucose, caused by mutation of the SGLT2 (or caused by use of SGLT2i)
  - <u>Cystinuria</u> - mutation of SLC3A1 amino acid transporter gene resulting in defective reabsorption of cystine, ornithin, arginine and lysine (predisposes to cystine stone formation)
  - <u>Hereditary hypophosphataemic rickets</u> - mutation resulting in defective reabsorption of filtered phosphates
  - <u>Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus</u> - failure of tubular cells to respond to vasopressin resulting in free water loss caused by inherited or acquired causes
  - <u>Bartter's and Gitelman's syndromes</u> - results in salt-wasting and hypokalaemia
  - <u>Fanconi syndrome</u> - generalised proximal tubular dysfunction resulting in hypophosphataemia, hypouricaemia, glycosuria, aminoaciduiria, and proximal renal tubular acidosis (a/w Wilson's disease. cystinosis, hereditary fructose intolerance)
  - <u>Renal tubular acidosis</u> (Type 1, 2 and 4) - common end-point of many tubulo-interstitial disease

## Diseases of the collecting system and ureters

### Congenital abnormalities

- **Various congenital abnormalities of the kidneys:**
  - Single kidney
  - Ureterocele
  - Ectopic ureters and duplex kidney
  - Ureterocele
  - Obstructive megaureter
  - Pelvi-ureteric junction obstruction

  
  ![](img/congenital_abnormalities_of_the_urinary_tract.png)
- **Pelvico-ureteric junction obstruction:**
  - **Definition** - congenital, often bilateral functional obstruction at the junction between the renal pelvis and ureters, resulting in idiopathic hydronephrosis
  - **Clinical features** - rarely asymptomatic:
    - <u>Loin pain</u> - ill-defined renal pain or ache, exacerbated by drinking large volumes of fluid (Dietl's crisis)
  - **Ix** - Dx by Tc-MAG3 renogram following diuretics
  - **Mx** - surgical excision of PUJ and re-anastamosis (pyeloplasty)

### Retroperitoneal fibrosis

- **Definition** - fibrosis of the retroperitoneal connective tissue that may encircle and compress on the ureter(s), causing ureteric obstruction
- **Etiology of retroperitoneal fibrosis:**
  - Idiopathic
  - Radiation
  - Aortic aneurysm
  - Malignancy
  - Drug (e.g. methysergide)
  - Infection
- **Clinical features of retroperitoneal fibrosis:**
  - Ill-defined symptoms of ureteric obstruction (e.g. loin discomfort)
- **Ix:**
  - <u>CRP/ESR</u> - elevated suggesting an acute phase response
  - <u>CT urogram</u> - shows ureteric obstruction and **medial deviation of ureters**
- **Mx:**
  - <u>Corticosteroids</u> - effective for idiopathic retroperitoneal fibrosis
  - <u>JJ stenting</u> - typically required to relief the obstruction

## Infections of the urinary tract

- **Bacterial colonisation in urinary tract** - confined to lower ends of urethra while others are sterile

### Urinary Tract Infections

- **Definition** - term to describe acute urethritis and cystitis caused by a microorganism
- **Epidemiology** - accounts for 1-3% of consultation:
  - Prevalence in F - 3% at age of 20, increased by 1% in each subsequent decade
  - Prevalence in M - uncommon, unless in first year of life or M \> 60y due to BOO
- **Pathophysiology of UTIs:**
  - **Lower tract balances factors for bacterial culture:**
    - Factors promoting growth - urine as a good culture medium, urothelium of susceptible person
  - **Route of infection:**
    - Ascending infection - from perineum to bladder (easier in F \> M due to shorter urethra and absence of bactericidal prostatic secretions), precipitated by urethral traumas (e.g. from sexual intercourse)
    - Innoculation - instrumentation of the bladder
    - Other sources of infection - descending infection (renal TB), haematogenous spread, lymphogenous spread, direct spread
  - **Pathogen-host-relationship determines host response:**
    - Pathogen factors - virulence factor, size of innoculum
    - Host factors - local and systemic factors of the patient
- **Risk factors of UTIs:** 
![](img/rfs_UTI.png)
- **Clinical features of UTI:**
  - **Frequency and urgency** - typically abrupt in onset
  - **Dysuria** - burning, scalding pain in the urethra during micturation
  - **Suprapubic pain** - typically during and after voiding
  - **Sense of incomplete voiding** (Strangury) - intense desire to pass more urine due to spasms of inflammed bowel wall
  - **Cloudy offending urine** - due to pyuria
  - **Haematuria** - may be microscopic or gross
  - **Absence of systemic features** - presence of which may suggest a complicated UTI:
    - <u>Fever, chills, rigors</u> - acute pyelonephritis complicated with septicaemia
    - <u>Loin pain with guarding and tenderness</u> - acute pyelonephritis
    - <u>Hypotension</u> - septic shock
- **DDx of UTI:**
  - Urethritis from sexually transmitted diseases - e.g. Chlamydia
  - Reiter's syndrome
  - Frequency-Dysuria syndrome - presence of urethritis and cystitis-like syndrome but negative urine culture
  - Non-infective lower urinary tract pathologies - interstital cystitis, ketamine cystitis, bladder stones
- **Ix:**
  - **Urinalysis** - indicated for all patients:
    - Dipstick - for nitrites, leukocyte esterase and glucose (markers of UTI)
    - Urine microscopy (WBC and organisms) and urine culture
  - **Additional Ix for complicated infections:**
    - Routine blood tests:
      - CBC - leukocytosis
      - RFT - renal insufficiency
    - Blood cultures - for septicaemia
    - US kidneys, CT urogram
- **Mx:**
  - Adequate hydration - \> 2L/d (not evidence-based and may make dysuria worse)
  - ABx - empirical Tx pending microbiological results results:
    - Regimen - PO nitrofurantoin, or augmentin (only if demonstrated sensative) 7d

### Asymptomatic bacteriuria

- **Definition** - presence of \> 10<sup>5</sup> organisms / mL in urine in an otherwise healthy individuals
- **Epidemiology** - 30% will develop symptoms after 1y:
  - <u>Prevalence</u>:
    - Children \< 1y - 1%
    - Schoolgirls - 1%
    - Schoolboys - 0.03%
    - Men - 1%
    - Non-pregnant women - 3%
    - Pregnant women - 5%
    - Long-term catheterisation - 100% have florid G- bacteria
- **Indications of Tx** - infants, pregnant patients, urinary tract abnormalities, pending urological procedures where mucosal bleeding is anticipated

### Tuberculosis of the kidney and urinary tract

- **Pathophysiology of genitourinary tuberculosis:**
  - Haematogenous spread - from other sites of infection, likely pulmonary TB
  - Descending infection - initial lesions in the renal cortex, which ulcerate and descend through the renal pelvis, ureters, bladder, epididymis, seminal vesicles and bladder
- **Clinical features of genitourinary TB** - important DDx of urothelial tumours:
  - Clinical course - insiduous onset with non-specific Sx
  - **Features of cystitis:**
    - Frequency, urgency, dysuria
    - Haematuria - ocassionally macroscopic
  - **Systemic features** - fever, night sweats, lassitude, weight loss
- **Ix:**
  - Urinalysis - sterile pyuria and haematuria
  - AFB smear, culture, PCR of early morning urine specimen
  - Imaging:
    - USG or CTU
    - CXR

## Benign Prostatic Enlargement

## Tumours of the kidney and urinary tract

### Renal Cell Carcinoma

- **Definition** - most common primary renal neoplasm arising from renal tubular cells
- **Epidemiology:**
  - Prevalence - 16/100000
  - Demographic - risk increases w/ age (most prevalent in 65-75y, uncommon \< 40y), and male preponderance (M:F = 2:1)
- **Risk factors of RCC:**
  - Smoking
  - Acquired polycystic kidney disease (related to chronic dialysis)
  - Carcinogen exposure:
    - Occupational exposure to benzene, cadmium, asbestos, petroleum-by-products
    - Cytotoxic chemotherapy
    - Prior irradiation
    - FHx - Von-Hippel-Lindau disease, Hereditary papillary RCC, Tuberous sclerosis, Hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cancer syndrome, Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome
- **Histological subtypes of RCC:**
  - Clear cell carcinomas (85%) - early direct spread towards hilum (renal veins and renal pelvis, resulting in gross haematuria)
  - Papillary
  - Chromophobe
  - Collecting duct
- **Mode of spread of RCC:**
  - Direct spread - to renal hilum and perinephric tissue
  - Lymphatic spread - to para-aortic LN
  - Haematogenous spread - lung, bone, brains
- **Clinical features of RCC:**
  - **Asymptomatic finding** (50%) - incidental finding on cross-sectional imaging for other reasons
  - **Constitutional symptoms** - Weight loss, fever, anaemic symptoms
  - **Effects of primary tumour** - presents as triad (10%) of haematuria, loin pain, and mass:
    - <u>Haematuria</u> (60%) - initially intermittent and microscopic, but can become macroscopic subsequently
    - <u>Flank pain</u> (40%) - typically a dragging discomfort, may be colicky if caused by clot colic obstructing the PUJ
    - <u>Mass</u> (25%) - non-tender, uniform, and moves with respiration
  - **Effects of locoregional spread** - due to propensity of venous invasion:
    - <u>Scrotal varicoceles</u> - classically left side due to compression of gonadal veins
    - <u>IVC obstruction</u> - oedema, ascites, PE, dilated veins on abdomen
  - **Paraneoplastic syndrome:**
    - Pyrexia of unknown origin (20%) - intermittent, and a/w night sweats, anorexia, and weight loss
    - Anaemia (29-88%) - a disproportionately severe NcNc anaemia with iron profiles compatible that of anaemia of chronic disease (ACD)
    - Polycythaemia (1-5%) - secretion of erythopoietin
    - Hypercalcaemia (15%) - PTHrP production or lytic bone metastasis
    - HTN - renin secretion
    - Hypercortisolism (Cushing's syndrome) - ACTH secretion
    - Hypoglycaemia - secretion of insulin
  - Features of metastasis - typically lungs, LNs, bone, liver and brain
- **Ix and diagnostic evaluation:**
  - <u>Imaging</u> - US for clinical suspicion, CT abdomen, IVU (being superceded by CT):
    - **Ultrasound** - typically differentiation between simple cysts and solid tumours
    - **Contrast CT abdomen (renal protocol)** - characteristic findings with high diagnostic accuracy:
      - <u>Size</u> - \> 4cm has high PPV for RCC
      - <u>Description</u> - heterogenous lesion comprising of solid, cystic, necrotic, haemorrhagic areas resulting in variable enhancement, with <u>distortion of renal outlines</u>
      - <u>Enhancement</u> - 10-15 HU hyper-enhancement on post-contrast phase

      
      ![](img/rcc_ct.png)
  - <u>Laboratory studies</u>:
    - Urinalysis - shows microscopic haematuria
    - CBC - Abnormal Hb (anaemia or polycythaemia) +/- leukocytosis and thrombocytosis (?secondary PV)
    - RFT - ?renal insufficiency
    - LFT - dLFT if liver metastasis, ALP reflects bone metastasis
    - CaPO4 - HyperCa due to PTHrP or lytic bone metastasis
  - <u>CT core biopsy</u> - rarely indicated due to risk of transperitoneal seeding
- **Mx of RCC:**
  - **Surgical Tx** - indicated for localised diseases:
    - <u>Partial nephrectomy</u> - removal of tumour with tumour free margins, recommended for small tumours (\< 4cm) due to lower risk of cardiac and renal morbidities
    - <u>Radical nephrectomy</u> - removal of kidney, perirenal fsacia envelope and ipsilateral para-aortic LN, indicated for most RCCs
    - +/- <u>Resection of solitary metastasis</u> - can remain single for long periods and excision may be worthwhile
  - **Ablation** - indicated for localised diseases w/ high surgical risks:
    - Radiofrequency ablation
    - Cryotherapy
  - **Chemotherapy or immunotherapy** - indicated for <u>metastatic diseases</u> (RCC is chemo-resistant):
    - <u>Cytokine therapy</u> - INF, IL-2
    - <u>Tyroskine kinase inhibitor</u> - sunitinib, pazopanib, sorafenib, axitinib
    - <u>mTOR inhibhitors</u> - temsirolimus, everolimus
    - <u>PD-1 inhibitors</u> - Pembrolizumab, nivolumab, ipilimumab
- **Prognosis:**
  - Localised RCC - 75% 5y survival
  - Metastatic RCC - 5% 5y survival

### Urothelial Carcinoma

- **Definition** - neoplasms arising from the transitional epithelium (urothelium) of the renal tract from the renal pelvis to urethra, most commonly affects the bladder
- **Epidemiology:**
  - Demographic - increasing incidence with age, male preponderance (M:F = 3-4:1)
- **Pathology and histopathology of urothelial carcinoma:**
  - <u>Gross pathology</u> - spectrum ranging from delicate papillary structures (a/w good prognosis) to solid ulcerating mass (a/w poor prognosis)
  - <u>Histopathology</u>:
    - Transitional cell carcinoma (most common)
    - Squamous cell carcinoma - squamous metaplasia of urothelium due to chronic irritation or inflammation by stones or schistosomiasis
- **Site of urothelial carcinomas:**
  - Bladder carcinoma - most common site
  - Upper tract urothelial carcinoma (UTUC) - disease of the elderly
- **Risk factors of urothelial carcinoma:**
  - Cigarette smoking
  - Industrial carcinogen exposure - benzenes, aldeehydes, aniline dyes (painter, printer etc.)
  - Risk factors for UTUC:
    - Aristolochia fanchi (Chinese herbs)
    - Aristolchii acid (Balkans)
    - Arsenic poisoning
    - Hereditary syndromes (HNPCC)
  - +/- chronic bladder stones or schistosomiasis for SCC variant
- **Clinical features of urothelial carcinoma:**
  - **Gross haematuria** (80%) - painless, visible haematuria is assumed to be caused by tumour until proven otherwise
  - **Dysuria or storage symptoms** - only seen if bladder is involved
  - +/- Sx of obstruction - depending on site of involvement
- **Signs of urothelial carcinoma** - P/E typically unremarkable:
  - Palpable mass on bimanual examination on very advanced disease
- **Ix and diagnostic evaluation:**
  - <u>Cytoscopy</u> - mandatory Ix in gross haematuria to visualise tumour:
    - Transurethral resection of bladder tumour - assessment of local staging, classifying into non-muscle invasive (MMI) and muscle invasive disease
  - <u>Upper tract imaging</u> (r/o other pathologies of upper tract) - CT urogram (gold standard), ureters and renal pelvis
  - <u>Retrograde pyelogram, ureteroscopy and Bx</u> - necessary for CTU suspicious of UTUC:
    - <u>Findings of retrograde pyelogram</u> - Goblet sign (cupping of injected contrast due to obstruction)
- **Staging Ix:**
  - **Transurethral resection of bladder tumour** (TURBT) - for local staging into NMI and invasive bladder cancer:
    - <u>Pathology returns muscle invasive CA</u> - proceed with staging and offer radical cystectomy as Mx
    - <u>Pathology returns NMI CA</u> - second-look TURBT to avoid understaging of disease

    
    ![](img/CA_bladder_local_staging.png)
  - **PET-CT/ CT T+A+P** - detection of metastasis in muscle invasive diseases

1.  Management

    - **Principles of Mx:**
      - <u>NMI bladder tumours</u> - transurethral endoscopic resection +/- intravesical chemotherapy withmitomycin C (+/- intravesical BCG therapy)
      - <u>Muscle invasive bladder tumours</u> - radical cystectomy with urinary diversion into incontinent ileal conduit or a continent catheterisable bowel pouch (or radiotherapy)
    - **Prognosis** - dependent of T stage and grading:
      - <u>Survival</u>:
        - Muscle invasive CA - 70% 5y survival
      - <u>Progression</u> - dependent in T stage and grading:
        - Low grade superficial bladder cancers - 5% progression into invasive bladder cancer
        - High-grade (HG) superficial bladder cancers - 50% progression into invasive bladder cancers
      - <u>Recurrence</u> - high for NMI CA:
        - Recurrence rate - 20-90% at 1y
    - **Follow-up:**
      - Regular surveillance cystoscopy - q3mo at 1y, q6mo at 2y,, q1y there-after

### Prostate Carcinoma

- **Definition** - neoplasm arising from the glandular epithelium in the peripheral zone of the prostate
- **Epidemiology** - most common malignancy in men in the west:
  - <u>Prevalence</u> (M) - 3rd in incidence, 4th in mortality
  - <u>Demographic</u> - mean age of presentation at 70y (rare in \< 50y)
- **Pathology and histopathology:**
  - Pathology - typically arises from peripheral zone of the prostate
  - Histopathology - adenocarcinomas
- **Route of spread:**
  - <u>Direct spread</u> (clinically and pathologically defined) - based on size, and extraprostatic extension, invasion into seminal vesicles, and adjacent structures (bladder, rectum, levator muscles, pelvic wall)
  - <u>Lymphatic spread</u> - to pelvic LNs
  - <u>Haematogenous spread</u> - to spine or bones
- **Clinical features of prostate cancer:**
  - <u>Asymptomatic</u> - incidentally worked up from elevated PSA
  - <u>Local Sx of prostate cancer</u> - Lower urinary tract Sx (indistiguishable from BPH) and haematuria
  - <u>Constitutional Sx</u> - weight loss (only in metastatic disease), anaemia
  - <u>Sx of distant metastasis</u> - bone pain, back pain, ureteric obstruction, LL weakness
- **Signs of prostate cancer** - 45% of CA prostate not palpable on DRE:
  - Nodular and hard
  - Loss of median groove
- **Initial Ix:**
  - **Serum PSA levels** - counsel patient of limitations of tests and implications of workup:
    - <u>Test properties</u> - organ-specific and non-cancer specific:
      - PSA 4-10 ng/dL - 40% have cancer on Bx
      - PSA \< 4 ng/dL - 25% have cancer
    - <u>Implications</u>:
      - PSA \< 4 ng/dL - does not r/o cancer
      - PSA \> 4 ng/dL - does not confirm diagnosis, but opens discussion for Bx
  - **Prostate Bx** (TRUS-Bx) - diagnostic for prostate cancer
- **Staging Ix:**
  - **Pelvic MRI** - assessment of presence and extent of loco-regional involvement
  - **Isotope bone scan** - indicated if distant bone metastasis is suspected:
    - <u>Indications</u> - clinically suspected bone metastasis, or high PSA levels (\> 100 ng/dL almost certain of metastasis)
  - **PSMA-PET scan** - detection of all sites of metastasis:
    - <u>Principles</u> - Radiolabelled prostate-specific membrane antigen binds to PSMA over-expressed on cancer cells
- **Mx:**
  - Organ-confined disease - radical prostatectomy, radical radiotherapy, or brachytherapy
  - Locally advanced disease - multimodal approach
  - Metastatic disease - Androgen deprivation therapy or upfront additional therapy

### Tessticular tumours

- **Definition** - various tumours arising from cells of the testes, most commonly seminoma and teratoma
- **Epidemiology:**
  - <u>Prevalence</u> - uncommon urological tumours (5 in 100000 M)
  - <u>Demographic</u> - typically presents between 20-40y (85% are seminomas or teratomas)
- **Classification of testicular tumours:**
  - Seminoma - arises from the seminiferous tubules
  - Teratomas - arises from primitive germinal cells
  - Leydig cell tumours - uncommon tumours arising from Leydig cells and typically secrete estrogen
- **Clinical features of testicular tumours:**
  - <u>Scrotal mass</u> - incidental discovery of a unilateral painless testicular lump, althou patients may complain of testicular ache
  - <u>Gynaecomastia</u> - only presents in those w/ estrogen-secreting Leydig cell tumours
- **Ix:**
  - <u>USG</u> - evaluation of the scrotal lump
  - <u>Hormone panel</u> - testosterone, FSH, LH, E2 for hormone-screting tumours
  - <u>Tumour markers</u> - AFP, beta-HCG
  - <u>CT T+A+P</u> - staging for distant metastasis in the lungs, liver and retroperitoneal area
- **Mx** - dependent on underlying histology
- **Prognosis:**
  - <u>Seminoma</u> - 90-95% 5y survival
  - <u>Teratoma</u> - 60-65% 5y survival depending on histology, stage and tumour volume

## Renal involvement in systemic conditions

### Diabetes mellitus

- **DM and renal diseases:**
  - Most common cause of CKD in developed countries
  - DM is associated w/ steady advancement from microalbuminuria to dipstick-positive proteinuria, where degree of proteinuria is prognostic of the rate of progression of CKD
  - Role of ACEi and SGLT2i in proteinuria control and halting progressive renal dysfunction

### Hepatic-renal syndrome

- **Hepatic-renal disease** - patients w/ severe hepatic dysfunction predisposes to pre-renal and renal AKI:
  - <u>Hypovolaemia</u> - as a result of variceal bleeding, over-diuresis etc.
  - <u>Hepato-renal syndrome</u> (Type 1 vs Type 2) - arterial underfilling leading to renal vasocosntriction
  - <u>Acute tubular necrosis</u> - reduced hepatic metabolic functions (e.g. against drugs) predisposes to renal injury to relatively minor insults
  - <u>IgA nephropathy</u> - IgA nephropathy being more common in patients with liver disease

### Pulmonary renal syndrome

- **Pulmonary-renal syndrome** - refers to <u>dramatic presentation w/ renal and respiratory failure</u> that cannot be explained by fluid overload or by severe pneumonia, including:
  - <u>Goodpasture syndrome</u> - anti-GBM disease w/ involvement of pulmonary basement membrane and GBM
  - <u>Small-vessel vasculitides</u> - typically affects both renal and pulmonary vasculature

### Maligant diseases

- **Malignant disease w/ renal manifestations:** 
![](img/renal_effects_of_malignancies.png)
  - <u>Primary malignancies of the urinary tract</u> - renal cell carcinoma, urothelial tumours
  - <u>Compressive effects on the urinary system by intra-abdominal tumours</u> - gynaecological malignancies, colorectal carcinoma, lymphoma
  - <u>Immune-mediated manifestations</u> - membranous nephropathy
  - <u>PTHrP-mediated hypercalcaemia of malignancy</u> - increased renal reabsorption of Ca
  - <u>Tumour lysis syndrome</u> - causes uric acid nephropathy
  - <u>Myeloma-defining events</u> - cast nephropathy

### Systemic vasculitis

- **Small vessel vasculitis w/ renal involvement:**
  - <u>Etiology</u>:
    - ANCA-associated vasculitis (MPA or WG)
    - Other connective tissue disorders (e.g. RA, SLE, cryoglobulinaemia)
  - <u>Pathopphysiology</u> - typically presents w/ rapidly progressive (crescentic glomerulonephritis)
  - <u>Mx</u>:
    - High-dose glucocorticoids w/ cyclophosphamide or mycophenolate mofetil
    - High-dose glucocorticoids w/ rituximab (RAVE study)
    - Plasmapharesis

    
    ![](img/role_of_rituximab_in_anca_associated_vasculitis.png)
- **Medium- to large-vessel vasculitis** (e.g. polyarteritis nodsa) - results in renovascular HTN, acute renal infarction +/- haematuria

### Systemic lupus erythromatosis

- **Clinical presentation of lupus nephritis** - clinically variable and can result in various histological pattern:
  - Chronic glomerulonephritis - subacute disease w/ inflammatory features, e.g. haematuria, HTN, variable renal impairment, and heavy proteinuria
  - Nephrotic syndrome
  - Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis

